.TH tuhttps n 0.1 tclutils
.SH tclutils::tuhttps
.PP
One place for the HTTPS policy of the modules that use the core \fBhttp\fR package with \fBtls\fR: \fBtufetch\fR, \fBtudav\fR, \fBtupostgrest\fR. Builds a \fBverifying\fR \fB::tls::socket\fR command and registers \fBhttps\fR only for the duration of a script.
.PP
Needs the \fBtls\fR package when \fBsocketCmd\fR is called (loaded lazily). Tcl 8.6+ and 9.x.
.SS "Why it exists"
.PP
\fB::http::register\fR is \fBglobal\fR to the interpreter. Up to tclutils 0.62.0 the three modules each registered \fBhttps\fR themselves, with three different policies, and never undid it — whoever registered last decided for everybody:
.TS
l l l.
module (≤ 0.62.0)	registered	verified?
\fBtufetch\fR 0.3	on every call	no (\fB\-require 0\fR)
\fBtupostgrest\fR 0.1	on every request	yes, unless \fB\-insecure 1\fR
\fBtudav\fR 0.1	once, for good	tls 2.0 yes; \fBtls 1.x no\fR
.TE
.PP
Measured 2026\-09\-19 against a local self\-signed server: after one \fBtufetch\fR call, every later \fB::http::geturl https://…\fR in the process accepted the forged certificate. Under Tcl 8.6 \fBtudav\fR never verified at all.
.SS API
.CS
::tclutils::tuhttps::socketCmd ?-insecure 0|1? ?-cafile path? ?-host name?
::tclutils::tuhttps::with prefix script
::tclutils::tuhttps::caFile
.CE
.IP \(bu
\fBsocketCmd\fR — returns a command prefix for \fB::http::register https 443 …\fR.
.RS 4
.IP \(bu
Default: verifies (\fB\-require 1\fR).
.IP \(bu
\fB\-cafile path\fR — verify against this CA bundle.
.IP \(bu
\fB\-insecure 1\fR — accept any certificate. The only way to \fB\-require 0\fR.
.IP \(bu
\fB\-host name\fR — the request's host; an IP literal gets no SNI, because some tls builds reject an IP as SNI name ("failed to use socket").
.RE
.IP \(bu
\fBwith prefix script\fR — runs \fBscript\fR in the caller's frame with \fBhttps\fR registered to \fBprefix\fR; afterwards the previous registration is restored (or removed, if there was none), also when the script raises an error. Returns the script's result. Do not use \fBreturn\fR inside the script.
.IP \(bu
\fBcaFile\fR — the CA bundle used for tls 1.x, or \fB""\fR.
.CS
set prefix [::tclutils::tuhttps::socketCmd -host example.org]
set tok [::tclutils::tuhttps::with $prefix {::http::geturl https://example.org/}]
.CE
.SS "tls 1.x and the CA store"
.PP
\fBtls\fR 2.0 (Tcl 9) uses the OpenSSL default paths and needs no file. \fBtls\fR 1.x (the usual package on Tcl 8.6, also on Windows) does \fBnot\fR find the system CA store: with \fB\-require 1\fR and no \fB\-cafile\fR, every real HTTPS request fails. For tls < 2.0 \fBsocketCmd\fR therefore looks for a bundle, in this order:
.IP [1]
\fB$env(SSL_CERT_FILE)\fR
.IP [2]
\fB/etc/ssl/certs/ca\-certificates.crt\fR (Debian, Ubuntu)
.IP [3]
\fB/etc/pki/tls/certs/ca\-bundle.crt\fR (RHEL, Fedora)
.IP [4]
\fB/etc/ssl/ca\-bundle.pem\fR (openSUSE)
.IP [5]
\fB/etc/ssl/cert.pem\fR (macOS, Alpine, BSD)
.IP [6]
\fB/usr/local/etc/openssl/cert.pem\fR
.PP
If none is found it raises \fBNOCA\fR, whose message names the way out (\fB\-cafile\fR, \fBSSL_CERT_FILE\fR). It does not fall back to "no verification". On Windows with tls 1.x, set \fBSSL_CERT_FILE\fR or pass \fB\-cafile\fR.
.PP
Not measured: tls 2.0 on Windows. Whether its default paths find a CA store there depends on the OpenSSL build. If not, the handshake fails loudly (\fBcertificate verify failed\fR), and \fB\-cafile\fR is the way out.
.SS Errors
.TS
l l.
errorcode	meaning
\fB{TCLUTILS TUHTTPS NOTLS}\fR	the \fBtls\fR package is not available
\fB{TCLUTILS TUHTTPS CAFILE}\fR	\fB\-cafile\fR is not a readable file
\fB{TCLUTILS TUHTTPS NOCA}\fR	tls < 2.0 and no CA bundle found
\fB{TCLUTILS COMMON OPTION <opt>}\fR	unknown option (message lists the known ones)
\fB{TCLUTILS COMMON BOOLEAN \-insecure}\fR	\fB\-insecure\fR is not a boolean
.TE
.SS "What it does not see"
.PP
The registration is scoped, not locked. A caller that registers \fBhttps\fR itself after \fBwith\fR has started, or an asynchronous \fB::http::geturl \-command …\fR the application started while a \fBwith\fR script runs, can still meet a different registration.
.SS Tests
.PP
\fBtests/tuhttps.test\fR runs a local HTTPS server in a child process (\fBtests/data/https\-server.tcl\fR, self\-signed certificate for localhost and 127.0.0.1 in \fBtests/data/\fR, test use only). Both directions are checked: rejected by default, accepted with the certificate as \fB\-cafile\fR or with \fB\-insecure 1\fR, and the registration restored — also after an error.
